ility to have these expectancies that  are either met that are fulfilled or  that are not fulfilled and there's some  famous music theorists in fact who have  developed models about this so if we  come back to the brain for a moment  there's another aspect of musical  pleasure that we haven't entirely talked  about so up until now we've talked about  this sort of strong experience of chills  and and pleasure but most of our  experience when listening to music  doesn't necessarily involve such strong  pleasure sometimes it does and then it's  it's particularly wonderful but often  times we listen to music and we just  value it we enjoy it we like it but it  doesn't necessarily produce such a  strong response and we wondered whether  the same neural system is involved in  that sort of experience as opposed to  the very strong sort of physiological  alteration that you get with chills and  so in this experiment again Valery Salim  pour was the student who did the project  and she came up with the idea that we  could essentially use iTunes as our  model so what do we mean well in iTunes  you get to listen to an excerpt of music  and then you decide whether you want to  download it or not and you have to spend  a certain amount of money to download it  right so what we did is we put people in  the scanner and we had them listen to  pieces of music in fact these are new  pieces of music that had never heard  before but they were in a genre that we  were pretty confident that they would  probably like at least some of them and  then they had to give her a dollar  rating as to how much money they'll be  willing to spend for that piece of music  and then what we did is we look in the  whole brain and we used the dollar  amount to investigate which regions are  changing their activity depending on how  much value is assigned the value being  the monetary amount and what we see is  that that same region that had shown you  before that we saw in in the other  experiments so it's the ventral stratum  similarly to here similarly to there so  in that case we see that this system is  not only responsible for pleasure itself  but also is responsible responsible for  the value that we assign to music and  then we took one additional step and for  this aspect of the project we got help  from Randy Mackintosh here from the  Rahman who has some very interesting  ways of analyzing the relationship  between activity in one part of the  brain and in another part of the brain  by looking at the coupling between them  so it's sort of similar to what Laurel  was talking about we're looking at how  the fluctuations in brain activity are  they synchronized or are they not  synchronized and if they're synchronized  it must be because those parts of the  brain are somehow interacting and so  when we looked to see what other parts  of the brain were active with the  striatum we saw the auditory cortex  which is shown here in a coronal section  so what this is telling us is that the  auditory parts of the brain are  interacting with the reward parts of the  brain more and more as the value  increases  so when you really like something and  you're enjoying it and you want to buy  it in this particular experiment then if  there's a lot of communication between  those two systems compared to when you  are not so enthralled by it and  therefore aren't willing to spend money  on it  and I think this finding is really quite  important it kind of ties together a lot  of the themes that I've talked about  tonight because I've shown you earlier  evidence that this cortical circuit  that's on the surface of the brain  that's the most highly evolved part of  the human brain and that's the area  those are the systems that are important  for perceiving music performing music  and also for making these predictions  right for the anticipation that allows  us to decide whether the end or the  continuation of something is what we  expect it or not and that system is  coupled 