ause  the brain controls the motor actions of  the muscles that allow you to produce  music but at the same time those motor  actions produce sounds which influence  subsequent motor actions so it's very  much a loop in that respect and it's a  very complex system of course I'm only  giving you kind of a character of it  this evening but here's a sort of a  close-up and don't worry there won't be  an exam at the end where you have to  remember the denominator but I do want  to call your attention to this area in  the temporal lobe here the auditory  cortex because that's where impulses  come in through the ear and then go  through the brainstem and end up in the  cortex there and then there are a series  of cortical circuits which are quite  complex  that interconnect the auditory areas  with regions that have to do with motor  control in particular the promoter  regions and areas of the prefrontal  cortex and I'll come back to this idea  that these circuits are essential in  enabling many of the functions that are  the reason why we're able to both  produce and perceive music now we want  to probe inside the brains of musicians  how do we do that there are various ways  Laurel Illustrated some very elegant  studies using EEG which allows you to  look at temporarily unfolding events  with great precision we in our lab at  the Montreal Neurological Institute have  used various techniques but in  particular magnetic resonance imaging  because that allows us to pinpoint the  location of brain areas that are active  during particular tasks that are of  interest and one such function of  interest is the performance of music and  so in order to understand the  performance of music you actually have  to get people to play music inside an  MRI scanner and I don't know how many of  you have been inside an MRI scanner but  it's it's a rather confined however we  have been able to work with some music  technology engineers to build us some  instruments and if we can go to the  video this will illustrate the basic  idea behind what we're trying to do is  to use music in a global way to  understand brain function  I look at what happens in the brain when  cello players are playing the cello  which is exciting to me because I am a  cello player and a neuroscientist  playing the cello inside of the MRI is  definitely very different than playing  the cello outside of the MRI for  starters you have to be lying down which  is a non-standard position for a chalice  to be in there are also the constraints  inherent in the instrument itself being  that it doesn't actually have a resonant  body so you don't get the vibration  feedback that you're used to getting  from your instrument it's also equipped  with gut strings which are a very  different feeling than the metal strings  they're much harder to get sound out of  so in this experiment for the first time  we're looking at how the brain of a  highly trained musician specifically a  cellist is working when they're playing  their instrument so this is a very cool  device that was actually built for us by  Ephraim Hollinger who is another person  who's somehow ended up in Toronto I  don't know why they all come here  so Avram built this device which allows  us to actually look at brain activity of  a professional cellist when they are  doing their thing and what we see when  we look at this pattern here so this is  showing you the green areas or areas  that are more active during the  performance of the instrument than  during other conditions and we see  indeed some of the same circuitry that  was shown here and I hasten to add that  this paper was published in 2007 and  this result was obtained only a couple  of months ago so I you know we weren't  sort of faking it  and the areas involved are of course the  auditory cortex and over here in the  parietal lobe and this frontal cortical  area and part of what this shows us with  some analysis of the interrelationship  between those regions is that these  regions are working together as a sort  of unit because when you're playing the  instrument you not only have to produce  the sound but you have to adjust the  position and the pressure of your motor  action in order to get just the right  pitch so the auditory and motor systems  are in constant interaction under those  kinds of conditions now in order to be  able to play an instrument if you take  highly trained musicians like we have on  stage here you have to undergo many many  years of practice and training and  another feature that we've been trying  to understand is how the brain changes  as a consequence of all that training so  one nice thing about MRI is that we can  not only look at brain activity so as  we're looking at there with functional  magnetic resonance image but we can also  look at brain anatomy so here are some  images of the amount of cortical  thickness that is different between  people with musical training and people  without this is from some work done by  Patrick Bermudez who actually wasn't  trying to became back to Montreal  is in our good books and what Patrick  discovered is that there are areas of  greater cortical thickness so the actual  amount of gray matter in the brain is  greater in the auditory areas here in  the right and in the left hemisphere as  well as in the motor cortex and also in  the frontal cortex so we interpret this  as evidence that there's a certain  degree of plasticity meaning that the  brain of a musician has adapted to be  able to do what they do so well and  indeed the regions that are involved are  many of the same ones that I've just  shown you are involved in the playing of  the instrument now another very  important feature is not only the amount  of practice that you have but also the  age at which you begin to train and not  only in the case of music but in many  other domains as well  sports and vision and so forth it is  shown that the early training is more  important because the development of the  brain has more plasticity than later on  in life and actually I'm just wondering  for our musicians I'd like to know what  ages they started their their musical  training so how old are you when you  first started taking lessons three and  Jamie five and Rowan  six so you were you were just you were  just on the edge there  now I started training as an organist  when I was 13 and this is why these guys  were on stage playing and I'm giving a  lecture and we know that these factors  play an important role because if we  look at the again the anatomy of the  brain as a function of the age at which  training began and this is controlling  for the total number of years of  training so it's just about the age at  which training began we see that there  are changes in some of those frontal  areas that I showed you in the previous  slide this is work done by and Bailey is  another another one who moved to Toronto  working in Virginia Benjamin's lab and  not only is there a change in the  cortical structure but also that  cortical structure allows you to perform  more synchronously so when the musicians  are playing together they are better  able to tap in synchrony or move in  synchrony if they had that earlier  training so some of those phenomena that  Laurel was talking about the degree of  extreme position that's necessary when  they play which you can hear is really  much easier to achieve if you begin  training early it's not impossible to  achieve if you begin later but it is  more difficult okay so that is a lot  about the performance aspect and I want  to move to a different feature that  involves some of the same brain systems  and that is the ability to imagine music  musical imagery it's the ability that we  have to imagine the sounds of music and  I've put Beethoven here because in a  little while at the end of the evening  we're going to hear the trio perform the  the archduke trio and this is actually  some of Beethoven sketches for that  piece and that piece was composed around  1811 and we know that Beethoven was  already quite deaf by then because in  1802  he wrote his famous Highlander sh'not  Testament basically a long letter where  he pours out his heart about the fact  that he's going deaf and he is even  considering suicide because it's so  difficult for him to consider this and  yet he persevered and what's remarkable  of course is that even at a point in his  life when he was really quite completely  deaf and there's very good documentation  of this he was able to compose  magnificent music which means that he  must have been able to imagine it  extremely clearly and well to be able to  write it down so that's really quite  remarkable now how do we study that in  lab well we have different sorts of  approaches but one of the one of the  approaches that we've use we're going to  try to simulate here in the in the  concert hall so what's going to happen  now is the following the musicians are  going to play an extremely well-known  tune that you will recognize instantly  but they won't play the whole thing  they'll play about the first half or so  and then they'll stop your job is soon  as they stop you have to imagine the  rest of the tune as it would normally  play out in the appropriate rhythm and  so forth you really need to just imagine  that sound in your mind's ear and when  you get to the end of the song okay at  the very end when the when you're  imagining in your imagination has  stopped then you press your clicker you  got that okay so the musicians will play  you'll stop halfway through and then you  continue in your mind and then you click  when you get to the very end and then  we're going to see when the clicking is  happening please  okay some of you were were asleep  maybe that's it there's you were  supposed to actually stop you weren't  meant to keep singing because it is kind  of you can if there's anyone that can't  get out of it you know so the idea here  is that hopefully most of you experience  the ability to imagine the continuation  of that song and what happens in our  experiments is that we actually scan the  brain during that silent period when  people are imagining something like that  when there's no actual sound but they  they're trying to recreate the sound in  their mind and when we look at the brain  activity associated with that we see the  following so in the here's a lateral  view of the brain again in the orange is  the auditory cortex which is active when  people are listening to real sound  that's the control condition when they  hear the actual music and then during  the silent period some of those same  regions are active so in the green  regions we see parts of the brain that  are active both for the real music and  in silence when you're imagining the  music so portions of the auditory cortex  which respond to real sound also respond  to internal sound which is really pretty  amazing because there's no actual sound  there and yet those neurons are active  in addition some of the same regions we  saw before in particular here the dorsal  premotor cortex that's typically very  much recruited during these kinds of  tasks because imagination is an active  task it actually requires we think a  motoric component as well as a  perceptual component and so this  circuitry that we're talking about is  really interesting because it is able to  be put to work not only for decoding  external stimuli but also for internal  recreation of previously experienced  information there's another feature that  I want to point out of this auditory  frontal Network and this relates to some  of the things that Laurel mentioned  which is the ability to  anticipate events so Laurel mentioned it  primarily in the context of rhythmic  events anticipating when something will  happen but it's also possible to  anticipate the nature of the event so  for example in this study carried out by  Marcin reasoner what we see is that both  auditory and frontal cortex respond to a  deviation from what's expected and this  sort of experiment you hear a sequence  of tones and then at some point a tone  is presented that was not one of the one  of the ones in the sequence previously  presented and so therefore it's a  deviation from the regular expected  pattern and the nervous system in  general is very sensitive to deviations  from regularity the important point here  is that you can't respond to a deviation  if you don't have a model of what it is  that's going to come in other words you  have an expectation you have a  prediction of what's going to come and  then what that what does come differs  from what was expected and that's the  deviation if you have no capacity to  generate that prediction you won't  notice anything that's deviant right so  we're going to come back to that topic  in the context of emotion in a couple of  minutes so I've been telling you about  these circuits which start in the  auditory cortex and of course the  auditory cortex is responsible for our  ability to perceive sound and our  ability to perceive relationships of  sound rhythms and harmonies and tonal  patterns and musical intervals and so on  and so forth and I've also talked to you  about these pathways that go from the  auditory cortex into other parts of the  brain and actually feed back eventually  back to the auditory cortex and these  loops so-called are important not only  for sound but also are enhanced by  training and are necessary for the  production of music so there's a very  nice parallel between production and  perception as well I've Illustrated  evidence that some of these same  circuits are important to enable our  ability to imagine  sounds and that these systems are highly  sensitive to regularities and therefore  are able to generate expectancies but  what does all that have to do with  pleasure because I promised you in the  title at least that I was going to tell  you something about pleasure and we've  already heard about emotion and music  and pleasure I would argue is a very  significant aspect of the emotional  impact that one gets from music in fact  one of the ways that we look at this is  by measuring when people experience a  strong pleasurable response to music so  that they get chills or sort of shivers  down the spine how many of you have  experienced that this evening it's quite  a few of you I'm sure  maybe when Julie hit that high note that  was that was one one moment for me I  think it was during Auto neopor tenure  and that's possible because I'm from  Argentina so I have a strong connection  to that music so when we talk about  pleasure we're actually talking about a  very different system than this cortical  system we're actually talking about a  system deep in the brain so in this  illustration here there's a cutout and  you're seeing some of these deep  structures basal ganglia and it's  actually a very complex system again I  emphasize these are highly  oversimplified sort of diagrams there's  a lot of other structures in the medial  frontal lobe and also in brainstem  nuclei but one of the most important  components of this system is the so  called striatum which actually consists  of two sub parts which we'll get back to  in a moment and these structures are  interesting because first of all they're  phylogenetically very ancient in the  sense that if you look in the brain of a  salamander you will see that they also  have Australia and similar in in  structure to ours and furthermore these  systems tend to be important for what  biologists have called stimuli or events  that are essential for survival of some  sort so for example they would respond  to a food reward so if you take a lab  rat and you  feed at a certain amount of food areas  in the stratum will respond because it's  a pleasurable sensation similarly these  regions have been shown to respond to  sexual stimulation and both food and sex  are obviously essential for survival  food for the survival of the individual  sex is obviously necessary for the  survival of species because without  reproduction you wouldn't have the  species and it's also been shown that  the same system responds to certain  kinds of drugs like and para means  cocaine alcohol part of the reason  they're so powerful and dangerous is  because they have a direct chemical  action on this system so a few years ago  we were wondering about music and why it  gives pleasure and we know from a vast  amount of literature that this is the  system that is associated with strong  pleasure and so we wondered could it be  that music would engage the same system  because after all music is nothing like  any of these other stimuli it's not a  substance it doesn't have a direct  chemical effect and strictly speaking  it's not even really necessary for  survival you might be miserable without  it but you wouldn't be dead without it  right so we've done a series of  experiments that show that indeed it is  the case that this area of the stratum  is active is responding to very strong  musical pleasure and we've done it in a  number of ways using a number of  different techniques these are all  vertical cuts through the brain like  this and you see the activity in these  deep structures ventral striatum here  also here and also here with different  measures in the older studies we did it  with just looking at cerebral blood flow  and more recent studies were able to  look at dopamine which is important  because dopamine is a neurotransmitter  and it is the neurotransmitter that is  most important in signaling reward and  many of those animal studies that I  mentioned that's the sort of molecular  signal that  there's a strong salient and pleasurable  stimulus there and I'll come back to  this third study in in just a second but  in this one which is carried out by  valerie salim poor one of my former PhD  students we noticed something which is  that they were actually sort of two  blobs you can probably see there's one  more in the upper part of the brain  dorsal striatum and one in the more  ventral region and we wondered why there  were those two responses and so with a  little bit of careful analysis Valerie  was able to demonstrate that there's two  separate phases of the response so one  of them is in the dorsal striatum and  one of them is in the ventral stratum  and they're divided according to the  moment of the peak pleasure so in these  experiments we have people listen to  music that they themselves have selected  as chill inducing so this is not hard to  do people have lots of recordings that  they say every time I hear this passage  there's a particular spot and oh my god  is just wonderful and when that happens  they indicate to us that it's happened  by pressing a button we can also measure  physiological responses like heart rate  and so on and what happens in the brain  is really quite interesting because in  the moments prior to that peak pleasure  there is a response here in the light  blue line in the dorsal stratum which is  this area and then at the moment of peak  pleasure it goes down whereas the  ventral striatum which is down here is  slightly active during that time but  then immediately when the peak pleasure  moment arises that's when it really  strongly shoots up and so this means  that there are actually two different  things going on one is the actual  experience of that pleasure when you  really feel that chill but on another  and I think extremely important one is  this anticipatory phase and this is I  think again related to some of the ideas  that Laurel told us about now we're  going to try to do a little experiment  here and probably if there's an ethicist  in the audience I'll be in trouble  because I haven't gotten in  form consent from you however we're  going to try it anyway and the idea here  is that I'm trying to have you  understand this idea of anticipating and  then receiving the element or the  stimulus that you expect based on what  came before it  so our trio has kindly agreed to play  the same segment of music with three  different endings okay and what I want  you to do is very simple you're going to  hear each of these three and I want you  to at the end after you've heard all of  them so just hold off until you've heard  everything at the end once you do a vote  for the one that you found most  pleasurable so this same piece of music  three different endings and at the end I  want you to use your clicker and decide  if it's number one number two or number  three and now no laughing this is an  experiment  now number three please okay now you get  to vote which of those three did you  find most pleasurable okay so that's  very interesting experiments don't  always work I think I just lost a beer  over this so the first the first one was  actually the conventional ending which  is to say it's the most predictable and  standard one the second one was slightly  non-conventional but actually still  within kind of a tonal structure and the  third one was completely unpredictable  essentially random set of notes that had  no relationship to what came before so  at the very least most of you except for  a few recalcitrant people and and maybe  you need to come to the lab later to  we'll check out your your auditory  cortex  so what's interesting here is I think  that this whole idea of predictability  is obviously playing an extremely  important role because you obviously  preferred the one that would had the  highest predictability which means that  you were able based on the first set of  notes to anticipate that that ending  should have a certain sound right so if  you haven't been able to do that you  wouldn't have shown any particular  preference and I think in the context of  music both the performance of it and the  composition of it musicians know this  musicians understand the idea of playing  around with the tension and the  resolution that's afforded by our  ability to have these expectancies that  are either met that are fulfilled or  that are not fulfilled and there's some  famous music theorists in fact who have  developed models about this so if we  come back to the brain for a moment  there's another aspect of musical  pleasure that we haven't entirely talked  about so up until now we've talked about  this sort of strong experience of chills  and and pleasure but most of our  experience when listening to music  doesn't necessarily involve such strong  pleasure sometimes it does and then it's  it's particularly wonderful but often  times we listen to music and we just  value it we enjoy it we like it but it  doesn't necessarily produce such a  strong response and we wondered whether  the same neural system is involved in  that sort of experience as opposed to  the very strong sort of physiological  alteration that you get with chills and  so in this experiment again Valery Salim  pour was the student who did the project  and she came up with the idea that we  could essentially use iTunes as our  model so what do we mean well in iTunes  you get to listen to an excerpt of music  and then you decide whether you want to  download it or not and you have to spend  a certain amount of money to download it  right so what we did is we put people in  the scanner and we had them listen to  pieces of music in fact these are new  pieces of music that had never heard  before but they were in a genre that we  were pretty confident that they would  probably like at least some of them and  then they had to give her a dollar  rating as to how much money they'll be  willing to spend for that piece of music  and then what we did is we look in the  whole brain and we used the dollar  amount to investigate which regions are  changing their activity depending on how  much value is assigned the value being  the monetary amount and what we see is  that that same region that had shown you  before that we saw in in the other  experiments so it's the ventral stratum  similarly to here similarly to there so  in that case we see that this system is  not only responsible for pleasure itself  but also is responsible responsible for  the value that we assign to music and  then we took one additional step and for  this aspect of the project we got help  from Randy Mackintosh here from the  Rahman who has some very interesting  ways of analyzing the relationship  between activity in one part of the  brain and in another part of the brain  by looking at the coupling between them  so it's sort of similar to what Laurel  was talking about we're looking at how  the fluctuations in brain activity are  they synchronized or are they not  synchronized and if they're synchronized  it must be because those parts of the  brain are somehow interacting and so  when we looked to see what other parts  of the brain were active with the  striatum we saw the auditory cortex  which is shown here in a coronal section  so what this is telling us is that the  auditory parts of the brain are  interacting with the reward parts of the  brain more and more as the value  increases  so when you really like something and  you're enjoying it and you want to buy  it in this particular experiment then if  there's a lot of communication between  those two systems compared to when you  are not so enthralled by it and  therefore aren't willing to spend money  on it  and I think this finding is really quite  important it kind of ties together a lot  of the themes that I've talked about  tonight because I've shown you earlier  evidence that this cortical circuit  that's on the surface of the brain  that's the most highly evolved part of  the human brain and that's the area  those are the systems that are important  for perceiving music performing music  and also for making these predictions  right for the anticipation that allows  us to decide whether the end or the  continuation of something is what we  expect it or not and that system is  coupled with the most phylogenetically  ancient system which is the reward  system which is key to our emotions and  it the idea that that we're sort of  working on at the moment is that this  interaction is really the reason why  music has such power in other words it's  bringing together the most highly  evolved parts of our brains that do  pattern recognition analysis of sounds  that are coming in predicting what's  going to happen next with the most sort  of strongly emotional parts of the brain  that have to do with the experience of  pleasure and that is probably why music  and perhaps other aesthetic experiences  are so powerful and why they're so  universal in our species and so I want  to leave you with that idea and just be  sure I thank all of the many people who  have contributed to this work all of my  colleagues some of whom are here this  evening  all of the students none of whom are  here because they're busy working in the  lab and again a special thanks to c4 and  the Israeli foundation for putting on  this whole show and to the funding  agencies that have been  generous with their support thank you so  much
 

